1595
00:40:00,960 --> 00:40:02,620
and further integrates them.

1596
00:40:02,960 --> 00:40:03,380
This is where

1597
00:40:03,380 --> 00:40:04,320
procedural memory,

1598
00:40:04,520 --> 00:40:05,720
like learning to ride a bike

1599
00:40:05,720 --> 00:40:06,500
or play the piano,

1600
00:40:06,620 --> 00:40:07,460
gets smoothed out.

1601
00:40:07,820 --> 00:40:08,480
And importantly,

1602
00:40:08,680 --> 00:40:10,220
it is where the emotional tone

1603
00:40:10,220 --> 00:40:11,860
of a memory is recalibrated.

1604
00:40:12,320 --> 00:40:13,400
Matthew Walker's work

1605
00:40:13,400 --> 00:40:14,160
on this is famous.

1606
00:40:14,420 --> 00:40:15,520
He talks about how we sleep

1607
00:40:15,520 --> 00:40:16,500
to remember the facts

1608
00:40:16,500 --> 00:40:17,040
of a trauma,

1609
00:40:17,500 --> 00:40:18,440
but we use REM sleep

1610
00:40:18,440 --> 00:40:19,160
to strip away

1611
00:40:19,160 --> 00:40:20,680
the raw visceral emotion

1612
00:40:20,680 --> 00:40:21,880
attached to those facts.

1613
00:40:22,140 --> 00:40:23,560
Because REM operates

1614
00:40:23,560 --> 00:40:24,660
under a totally different

1615
00:40:24,660 --> 00:40:25,740
neurochemical environment.

1616
00:40:26,780 --> 00:40:27,420
During REM,

1617
00:40:27,840 --> 00:40:28,920
your brain has almost

1618
00:40:28,920 --> 00:40:30,080
zero noradrenaline

1619
00:40:30,080 --> 00:40:30,780
the stress chemical.

1620
00:40:31,580 --> 00:40:32,500
It A is safe

1621
00:40:32,500 --> 00:40:33,680
neurochemical space.

1622
00:40:34,100 --> 00:40:35,620
It biases the continuation

1623
00:40:35,620 --> 00:40:36,360
of the memory

1624
00:40:36,360 --> 00:40:37,320
in a different way

1625
00:40:37,320 --> 00:40:38,320
than NREM does,

1626
00:40:38,640 --> 00:40:39,600
allowing you to process

1627
00:40:39,600 --> 00:40:40,800
highly emotional events

1628
00:40:40,800 --> 00:40:41,640
without the physical

1629
00:40:41,640 --> 00:40:42,520
stress response.

1630
00:40:42,900 --> 00:40:43,560
Okay, let me make sure

1631
00:40:43,560 --> 00:40:44,380
I'm really grasping

1632
00:40:44,380 --> 00:40:45,900
how different this is

1633
00:40:45,900 --> 00:40:47,000
from the hydraulic model.

1634
00:40:47,240 --> 00:40:48,480
If sleep was just

1635
00:40:48,480 --> 00:40:49,760
a hydraulic pressure release,

1636
00:40:50,140 --> 00:40:51,100
it would be like

1637
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:52,580
emptying your computer's

1638
00:40:52,580 --> 00:40:53,720
digital trash bin

1639
00:40:53,720 --> 00:40:55,660
to free up hard drive space.

1640
00:40:55,920 --> 00:40:57,080
It's a generic flush.

1641
00:40:57,140 --> 00:40:58,120
You couldn't selectively

1642
00:40:58,120 --> 00:40:59,540
save one specific file

1643
00:40:59,540 --> 00:41:00,440
while deleting another.

1644
00:41:00,780 --> 00:41:01,640
That is the perfect way

1645
00:41:01,640 --> 00:41:02,240
to look at it.

1646
00:41:02,240 --> 00:41:03,400
And the ultimate proof

1647
00:41:03,400 --> 00:41:04,160
that sleep isn't

1648
00:41:04,160 --> 00:41:05,260
just a generic flush

1649
00:41:05,260 --> 00:41:06,180
comes from something

1650
00:41:06,180 --> 00:41:07,160
called targeted memory

1651
00:41:07,160 --> 00:41:07,940
reactivation,

1652
00:41:08,400 --> 00:41:09,780
or TMR experiments.

1653
00:41:09,900 --> 00:41:11,220
Oh, this is the wild stuff

1654
00:41:11,220 --> 00:41:11,820
with the smells

1655
00:41:11,820 --> 00:41:12,600
and the sounds.

1656
00:41:12,960 --> 00:41:13,280
Yes.

1657
00:41:13,820 --> 00:41:15,240
Researchers like Bjorn Rash

1658
00:41:15,240 --> 00:41:16,160
and Jonathan Rudoy

1659
00:41:16,160 --> 00:41:17,560
did these incredible studies

1660
00:41:17,560 --> 00:41:18,960
that basically hacked

1661
00:41:18,960 --> 00:41:20,060
the admissibility gate.

1662
00:41:20,700 --> 00:41:21,520
In Rash's study,

1663
00:41:21,640 --> 00:41:22,740
people sat at a computer

1664
00:41:22,740 --> 00:41:23,900
and learned the locations

1665
00:41:23,900 --> 00:41:25,400
of card pairs on a screen,

1666
00:41:25,780 --> 00:41:26,740
like the game memory.

1667
00:41:27,220 --> 00:41:28,400
But while they were learning,

1668
00:41:28,780 --> 00:41:29,680
the researchers pumped

1669
00:41:29,680 --> 00:41:30,740
a very specific,

1670
00:41:30,960 --> 00:41:31,660
distinct odor,

1671
00:41:32,120 --> 00:41:33,080
the scent of roses,

1672
00:41:33,220 --> 00:41:33,780
into the room.

1673
00:41:34,440 --> 00:41:35,640
So their waking brains

1674
00:41:35,640 --> 00:41:37,000
subconsciously linked

1675
00:41:37,000 --> 00:41:38,300
the smell of roses

1676
00:41:38,300 --> 00:41:39,620
to the spatial memory

1677
00:41:39,620 --> 00:41:40,360
of the cards.

1678
00:41:40,480 --> 00:41:40,880
Exactly.

1679
00:41:41,300 --> 00:41:42,080
Then the subjects

1680
00:41:42,080 --> 00:41:42,700
went to sleep.

1681
00:41:43,180 --> 00:41:44,140
While they were in deep,

1682
00:41:44,320 --> 00:41:45,680
slow-wave NREM sleep,

1683
00:41:46,060 --> 00:41:46,920
the researchers pumped

1684
00:41:46,920 --> 00:41:48,420
that exact same rose odor

1685
00:41:48,420 --> 00:41:49,520
back into the room.

1686
00:41:49,680 --> 00:41:50,360
And what happened?

1687
00:41:50,440 --> 00:41:51,220
When they woke up,

1688
00:41:51,340 --> 00:41:52,240
their recall for those

1689
00:41:52,240 --> 00:41:53,480
specific card locations

1690
00:41:53,480 --> 00:41:55,300
was significantly improved

1691
00:41:55,300 --> 00:41:56,380
compared to control groups

1692
00:41:56,380 --> 00:41:57,280
who didn't get the smell

1693
00:41:57,280 --> 00:41:57,960
during sleep.

1694
00:41:58,320 --> 00:41:59,480
And Rudoy did the exact

1695
00:41:59,480 --> 00:42:00,120
same thing,

1696
00:42:00,200 --> 00:42:01,540
using specific sounds

1697
00:42:01,540 --> 00:42:02,780
associated with objects

1698
00:42:02,780 --> 00:42:03,360
on a screen.

1699
00:42:03,580 --> 00:42:05,300
So by playing a sound

1700
00:42:05,300 --> 00:42:06,580
or introducing a smell,

1701
00:42:06,760 --> 00:42:07,360
the researchers

1702
00:42:07,360 --> 00:42:08,960
were literally hijacking

1703
00:42:08,960 --> 00:42:10,640
the brain's admissions committee.

1704
00:42:11,160 --> 00:42:12,320
They were artificially

1705
00:42:12,320 --> 00:42:13,720
flagging a specific memory

1706
00:42:13,720 --> 00:42:14,160
and saying,

1707
00:42:14,300 --> 00:42:15,960
hey, prioritize this one

1708
00:42:15,960 --> 00:42:16,460
for the repair

1709
00:42:16,460 --> 00:42:17,920
and consolidation process.

1710
00:42:18,340 --> 00:42:19,320
If sleep was just

1711
00:42:19,320 --> 00:42:20,060
a pressure valve

1712
00:42:20,060 --> 00:42:21,140
flushing adenosine,

1713
00:42:21,620 --> 00:42:22,440
playing a sound

1714
00:42:22,440 --> 00:42:23,600
or pumping in a smell

1715
00:42:23,600 --> 00:42:25,140
wouldn't do absolutely anything

1716
00:42:25,140 --> 00:42:26,500
to save a specific file.

1717
00:42:26,720 --> 00:42:27,280
Precisely.

1718
00:42:27,280 --> 00:42:29,220
Sleep is a highly selective

1719
00:42:29,220 --> 00:42:30,540
active admissions committee.

1720
00:42:31,140 --> 00:42:32,340
It is actively deciding

1721
00:42:32,340 --> 00:42:33,480
which memories are fit

1722
00:42:33,480 --> 00:42:34,080
to continue

1723
00:42:34,080 --> 00:42:35,680
and shape your future behavior.

1724
00:42:36,340 --> 00:42:38,140
But Fliction does mention

1725
00:42:38,140 --> 00:42:39,200
the Tannoni-Sorelli

1726
00:42:39,200 --> 00:42:41,100
synaptic homeostasis hypothesis,

1727
00:42:41,340 --> 00:42:42,220
which is a big deal

1728
00:42:42,220 --> 00:42:42,980
in sleep science.

1729
00:42:43,360 --> 00:42:44,540
And that theory is essentially

1730
00:42:44,540 --> 00:42:46,180
a global downscaling theory.

1731
00:42:46,380 --> 00:42:46,740
It is.

1732
00:42:46,880 --> 00:42:47,940
The idea is that

1733
00:42:47,940 --> 00:42:48,600
during the day,

1734
00:42:48,660 --> 00:42:49,240
as we learn,

1735
00:42:49,340 --> 00:42:49,960
our synapses

1736
00:42:49,960 --> 00:42:51,020
get chemically stronger

1737
00:42:51,020 --> 00:42:51,500
and thicker.

1738
00:42:51,500 --> 00:42:52,640
And if they just kept

1739
00:42:52,640 --> 00:42:53,640
getting stronger forever,

1740
00:42:53,840 --> 00:42:54,760
our brains would literally

1741
00:42:54,760 --> 00:42:56,120
run out of physical space

1742
00:42:56,120 --> 00:42:56,660
and energy.

1743
00:42:57,120 --> 00:42:58,540
So sleep globally scales

1744
00:42:58,540 --> 00:42:59,360
all the synapses

1745
00:42:59,360 --> 00:43:00,520
back down to a baseline,

1746
00:43:00,760 --> 00:43:01,960
preserving the relative strength

1747
00:43:01,960 --> 00:43:02,860
of the important ones

1748
00:43:02,860 --> 00:43:03,600
while clearing out

1749
00:43:03,600 --> 00:43:04,360
the daily noise.

1750
00:43:04,720 --> 00:43:05,720
Which is a brilliant model

1751
00:43:05,720 --> 00:43:07,000
and it is very hydraulic

1752
00:43:07,000 --> 00:43:07,500
in nature.

1753
00:43:07,940 --> 00:43:08,900
It's about managing

1754
00:43:08,900 --> 00:43:09,960
the total energetic

1755
00:43:09,960 --> 00:43:11,020
and physical weight

1756
00:43:11,020 --> 00:43:11,940
of the synapses.

1757
00:43:12,520 --> 00:43:13,760
And Fliction doesn't dismiss this.

1758
00:43:13,820 --> 00:43:14,920
He says we have to stratify

1759
00:43:14,920 --> 00:43:15,780
our understanding.

1760
00:43:15,940 --> 00:43:16,180
Right.

1761
00:43:16,540 --> 00:43:17,200
Process S

1762
00:43:17,200 --> 00:43:18,380
and synaptic downscaling

1763
00:43:18,380 --> 00:43:19,920
are incredibly accurate

1764
00:43:19,920 --> 00:43:21,440
for tracking sleep deprivation

1765
00:43:21,440 --> 00:43:23,500
and the raw biological energy

1766
